Requires continuous memory bookkeeping overhead on the balancer. 
Furthermore, if a server experiences a quiet death and instantly 
rejects incoming traffic with a rapid 404 Error or TCP Reset, 
its connection count will drop to zero. The load balancer will view 
this broken server as highly available and stampede it with all new 
inbound traffic. it is known as The Black Hole Effect

ip hash:

this is also a static algo
computes a hash for the mathematical ip address and then the requests will land on the ssame server if the same user is trying to do something

least response time:
The load balancer continuously tracks milliseconds of latency using active 
ping cycles or live metrics. It actively shifts incoming requests to the 
fastest responding node.

but the catch here is it will overwhelm the fastest responding node. this is referred as the
sawtooth traffic patterns (Thundering Herd)

A load balancer is only as good as its health verification loops.
If your health check simply verifies a shallow TCP Ping on Port 80, the server will report HEALTHY even if its underlying database connection pool has completely crashed, causing it to return internal server error.


